          {
            title: 'Kláber',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'KlÃ¡ber (also known as FelsÅ‘s or Tartli) is the Hungarian variant of the trick-taking game Klaberjass, particularly popular among ethnic Hungarians in Hungary and neighboring regions. Unlike the international two-player version, KlÃ¡ber is primarily played by four people in fixed partnerships. It features a unique Hungarian deck with seasons-based Ace imagery and William Tell characters on the knaves (Over and Under).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Rooted in the 19th-century Central European 'Jass' family. It arrived in Hungary via the Austro-Hungarian empire and was adapted to the 32-card Magyar KÃ¡rtya (Hungarian cards).",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in fixed partnerships (2 vs 2).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32-card Hungarian deck (ranks: 7, 8, 9, 10, AlsÃ³/Under, FelsÅ‘/Over, King, Ace).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32-card Hungarian (Magyar Kártya) deck; scoring slate and chalk or equivalent.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards are dealt counter-clockwise in batches (3-2-3 or 4-4). Each player receives 8 cards.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Kontra / Rekontra / Szubkontra: Before play, defenders may say kontra to double hand score; contractor may rekontra; defenders may szubkontra (8×). Does not apply on throw-in hands.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Throw-in melds: If any player holds a meld worth 100+ points (four of a kind or sequence of 5+), they must declare; hand is thrown in and meld points scored. Concealment penalized: opponents score 161.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Maccs (double game): If a team reaches 501 when opponents have less than 251, winners score a double game (maccs).',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
